<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mehmed III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1566">1566</a>–<a href="/years/1603">1603</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mehmed III was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1595 until his death in 1603. Born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a> in 1566 to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-iii">Murad III</a> and his concubine Safiye, he had all nineteen of his brothers executed on his accession, while his mother Safiye <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> held such power as valide sultan that ministers regarded her as a co-ruler. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-turkish-war">Long Turkish War</a> he took personal command of the army, the first sultan to do so since Suleiman I, conquering Eger in 1596 and winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-keresztes">Battle of Keresztes</a>, though Győr was later lost. He had his son Mahmud strangled in 1603 and died that December at 37, succeeded by his 13-year-old son Ahmed I.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Princes governed provinces until their father died and then fought for the throne, and under Mehmed II the law allowed the victor to put his brothers to death: <b>Mehmed III</b> had nineteen executed in 1595.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
